## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world concerns: consent governance, dynamic schema evolution, cross-region data lineage, residency, and auditable privacy controls.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant consent governance and field-level filtering
- Edge filtering and region-local routing
- Schema evolution via Avro + schema registry with versioning
- Tamper-evident, immutable audit logs for lineage
- DSAR testing, data portability, and deletion guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
function filterFieldsByConsent(event, consentMap) {
  const allowed = consentMap[event.tenant_id] || [];
  const filtered = {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(event)) {
    if (allowed.includes(k)) filtered[k] = event[k];
  }
  return filtered;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle consent updates mid-stream without data loss?
- What metrics validate data minimization and retention policy?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical thinking on per-tenant privacy, simple policy evaluation, and a lean streaming path. It tests ability to reason about data models, minimal latency, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policies
- Ingest-time policy evaluation
- Event schema with tenant partitioning
- Data privacy and auditability
- Simple test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight policy evaluation skeleton
function evaluatePolicy(event, policyStore) {
  // policyStore: { tenant_id: { redact: [fields] } }
  const p = policyStore[event.tenant_id] || { redact: [] };
  const redacted = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(event.payload || {}));
  p.redact.forEach(f => { if (redacted.hasOwnProperty(f)) redacted[f] = "***"; });
  return { ...event, payload: redacted };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic policy updates without downtime?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure privacy and latency goals?
